Durarara!! AU
Chapter 13 – Part 1
Curtis left Russia Sushi with the late afternoon light slanting across the street, the conversation with the van gang and Masaomi already filed away behind him. Long black braids shifted against the blue uniform jacket as he walked without particular urgency, hands in his pockets, mismatched eyes half-lidded against the brightness.
He made it less than two blocks before a familiar figure stepped into his peripheral vision.
Anri Sonohara.
She had clearly been walking with no fixed destination—pace unhurried, path slightly irregular, the kind of aimless route someone took when they were hoping the city might arrange a coincidence. When she saw him, she slowed, then stopped, adjusting the strap of her bag with quiet composure that almost hid the fact that she had been looking for him at all.
Curtis came to a stop as well, brows lifting slightly.
“I thought you were with Mikado at the campus?”
Anri glanced aside for half a second before answering, voice soft and even.
“…No. He stayed behind. Masaomi left with you, and I… didn’t feel like walking the same way.” A small pause. “I decided to take the longer route home.”
She didn’t mention that the longer route had no real direction, or that she had been half-hoping the streets might produce exactly this meeting. She simply stood there in the afternoon light, black bob neat, glasses catching the sun, watching him with the same quiet attention she had given him in the hallway after Nasujima and again when he had spoken about Mika.
“Are you heading somewhere?” she asked.
Curtis accepted her answer at face value, even if the timing of their meeting felt a little too convenient to be pure chance. He didn’t call it out. He simply shifted his weight, long black braids moving against the blue uniform jacket, and glanced down the street.
“Well, the evening’s still young and it’s not too late. Wanna hang out?” His tone stayed easy, unforced. “I’m about to go grab some milk tea at a spot I found on my phone—one of those lists for tourists and first-timers like myself.”
Anri blinked once, the soft composure she usually wore flickering with something closer to quiet surprise. For a second she looked like she might decline out of habit—the automatic instinct to keep her space, to not impose, to return to the empty quiet of her usual route home.
Then she adjusted the strap of her bag and gave a small nod.
“…Okay.” The word came out softer than usual, but steady. “I don’t have anywhere I need to be right away.”
A faint pause. She didn’t mention that she had already abandoned the direct path home the moment she left the school gates, or that running into him had been the quiet hope underneath the wandering. She simply fell into step when he started walking, black bob neat in the late light, glasses catching the sun as they moved together down the sidewalk.
“What’s the place called?” she asked after a few steps, voice still quiet but no longer quite as guarded.
Curtis glanced at his phone once to confirm, then pocketed it again.
“It’s called Chatime. One of the ones that shows up on every first-timer list. Fancy enough that the drinks have too many steps, but the reviews said the milk tea’s actually good.”
Anri’s steps slowed almost imperceptibly.
“…I’ve seen that place,” she said quietly. “It’s a little expensive.” A small, careful pause. “I don’t really have the money for it right now.”
Curtis let out a short, low chuckle—more amused than anything else—and kept walking at an easy pace so she wouldn’t feel rushed.
“I wouldn’t invite you if I didn’t intend to pay.” He glanced sideways at her, mismatched eyes calm. “That’s not how I am.”
Anri looked at him for a moment, the usual soft reserve in her expression shifting into something a little more uncertain, a little warmer.
“…You really don’t have to,” she said. Then, quieter, almost like she was testing the words, “But… thank you. I’d like that.”
She adjusted her bag strap again and matched his pace more evenly, the late afternoon light catching on her glasses as they continued toward the shop. For the first time since leaving the school gates, the aimless feeling in her chest had somewhere solid to land.
They walked another half block before Anri spoke again, her voice careful in the way it always became when the subject turned toward the stranger parts of the city.
“…Have you heard of the Black Rider?”
Curtis glanced sideways at her, long black braids shifting with the motion. He gave a small nod.
“Yeah. I’ve heard the rumors.”
He left it there. No mention of yellow helmets, headless women, Shinra’s apartment, or the fact that he could hear a voice no one else could. Just the surface acknowledgment any new arrival in Ikebukuro would eventually collect.
Anri accepted the limited answer without pushing. She was quiet for a few more steps, then reached into her bag and pulled out her phone.
“There’s been talk about someone else lately,” she said. “People are calling them the Tech Rider. Or something close to that.” She unlocked the screen and held it out so he could see. “One person was seen riding around at night. Custom helmet. Glowing visor. Looks… expensive. Not like the usual bikes in the city.”
Curtis tilted his head slightly, mismatched eyes moving over the slightly blurry social media posts she had pulled up—red motorcycle, angular black-and-white helmet with a circular glowing visor, the rider’s silhouette sharp under streetlights. He gave no sign of recognition.
“…Never heard the name,” he said honestly. “And I haven’t seen anyone like that around.”
He handed the mental image back without comment, expression unchanged. Anri watched his face for a second longer than necessary, then locked her phone and slipped it away again. She didn’t mention the alley. She didn’t mention the white smoke, the filtered voice, or the way that same rider had pulled her out of a fight and asked for nothing in return.
She simply walked beside him toward the milk tea shop, carrying two separate versions of the same man and still having no reason to believe they were connected.
Curtis gave a small shrug, long black braids shifting against the blue uniform jacket as they continued walking.
“To be honest, I’m just trying to stay out of trouble and make it through college.” A short pause. “Although I gotta say—there are some interesting things that go on here that remind me of home.”
Anri glanced at him, the question forming before she could stop it.
“…You’ve never really said where home is.”
Curtis was quiet for a few steps. The caution sat clearly behind his eyes—he had already given Shinra, Celty, and Shizuo more than he meant to. The scars. The forest. The repeated near-deaths. The headless women. He was not going to make the same mistake twice in one week.
“Appalachia,” he said eventually. “I grew up out there. Woods. Small roads. Quiet in a way this city never is.” He left the cryptids, the fae, the spirits, and everything that had tried to kill him out of the sentence entirely. “Traveled around for a while after that. Ended up here.”
Another short pause. His voice stayed even, almost casual.
“Had a friend named Justice back then. We used to talk about getting out and seeing places like this someday.”
He didn’t say that Justice was gone. He didn’t say how. He simply let the name exist in the air between them, unfinished and carefully sealed, the same way he had sealed everything else.
Anri watched him for a moment, black bob neat in the late light, expression soft but attentive.
“…Appalachia,” she repeated quietly, as if testing the shape of the word. “That’s a long way from Ikebukuro.” A small breath. “And Justice… you talk about him like he’s still part of why you move the way you do.”
She didn’t press for the missing pieces. She simply held the limited version he had offered, the same way she held most fragile things—carefully, without demanding more than she was given.
“Thank you for telling me even that much,” she said. “You don’t have to share everything just because I asked.”
They walked a little further before Anri spoke again, her voice quieter than usual.
“…Thank you,” she said. “For the other day. With those girls and the one in the yellow hoodie. And for stepping in with Nasujima-sensei. And for putting your hand up for class rep with me.”
She kept her eyes on the sidewalk for a moment, then added, almost under her breath:
“I was hoping a cute boy would raise his hand.”
The meaning was clear. She didn’t look at him when she said it.
Curtis let out a low, short chuckle, long black braids shifting as he glanced sideways at her.
“Well… apologies for not filling the ‘cute’ part.”
Anri’s cheeks warmed. She gave a small, flustered shake of her head.
“That’s not— I didn’t mean it like that. I’m just… glad it was you.”
A brief silence settled between them. Then her expression turned more serious. She looked at him properly this time, the memory of the street still clear behind her eyes.
“…Are you okay?” she asked quietly. “From the other day. I was right there. I saw them hit you with the board… and I saw Izaya stab you. Twice.” Her gaze flicked briefly to his shoulder, then back to his face. “You were bleeding so much. And then you just walked into class the next morning like none of it happened.”
She didn’t push. She simply left the question there, soft but steady, still carrying the image of him standing in the middle of the street with blood running down his neck and shoulder while refusing to fall.
Curtis was quiet for a moment, mismatched eyes drifting toward the street ahead as if the memory of that afternoon was still sitting close to the surface.
“…Yeah.” He gave a small, rough exhale. “Sorry for losing my cool. And thank you—for pulling me back before Shizuo and I wrecked the street.”
The words were simple, but they carried weight. He remembered the exact moment her voice had cut through the tension, soft and strained, when he and Shizuo had been standing nearly chest-to-chest with the asphalt already cracked beneath them.
Anri’s steps slowed slightly. She looked at him, surprised by the directness of the thanks, then shook her head once.
“You don’t have to apologize for that,” she said quietly. “They came back with a weapon. And Izaya… he stabbed you while you were still holding onto him. Anyone would have lost their temper.”
A small pause. Her voice softened further.
“I just didn’t want to watch either of you destroy the street—or each other—right in front of me. I’m glad you stopped.”
She glanced at his shoulder again, then away.
“Does it still hurt?”
Curtis shook his head, the corner of his mouth lifting slightly.
“No. The doc used the good kind of duct tape.”
Anri let out a quiet chuckle before she could stop herself, the sound soft and surprised. Some of the remaining tension in her shoulders eased at the dry delivery.
Curtis’s smile lingered, small but genuine, as he glanced sideways at her.
“Nah. I got a few stitches, but other than that I’m in no pain, Anri-chan.”
Anri’s expression warmed at the casual way he said her name. She looked at him for a moment longer, as if still checking for any sign he was downplaying it, then gave a small, relieved nod.
“…Good.” Her voice was quieter, but lighter than before. “I’m glad. You scared me that day. Standing there bleeding and still acting like the biggest problem was your shirt.”
A faint, almost shy smile pulled at her mouth.
“Just… try not to collect any more stitches for a while, okay?”
Curtis grimaced faintly, as if only now remembering the practical aftermath.
“Oh right. I still gotta replace the ruined shirt and pants.” He gave a small shrug. “And I’m fine, Anri. I’ve been through worse.”
He almost continued. The words about the alley—the black hoodie, the cut across Celty’s arm, the white smoke, the way he had pulled her out—sat on the edge of his tongue for half a second. Anri tilted her head slightly, waiting, sensing there was more.
Curtis shook his head once.
“…It’s nothing.”
They reached Chatime. The interior was bright and modern, the air sweet with milk and sugar. Curtis ordered first, then waited while Anri chose hers. When the total came up he paid without comment, took both drinks, and led them to a quiet table near the window.
They sat.
Anri wrapped her hands around her cup, letting the warmth settle for a moment before she asked, carefully:
“…Do you live with your parents?”
Curtis’s expression didn’t change much, but something behind his mismatched eyes went more distant.
“My parents…” He shook his head. “No. I haven’t lived with them for years. They… abandoned me when I was five.” A short, even pause. “I tend not to tell people that. Not because it’s a secret, exactly. People just get all… I don’t know how to put it. It’s not weird to be sad about someone being abandoned. I just don’t feel that way. Life got way better after.”
Anri watched him quietly. The usual soft reserve in her face had thinned into something more attentive, more careful.
“…Were you abused?” she asked gently.
Curtis nodded once, small and without drama.
“I didn’t know it was abuse until much later in life.”
The admission sat between them, plain and unadorned. He didn’t elaborate. He simply left it there, the same way he left most heavy truths—offered without performance, and without asking for anything in return.
Anri’s fingers tightened slightly around her cup. For a moment she didn’t speak, the weight of what he had just given her settling carefully behind her eyes.
Anri was quiet for a long moment after he nodded.
Then, carefully, she reached across the small table and rested her hand over his. The contact was light, almost hesitant, but she didn’t pull away.
“…I understand,” she said softly.
She kept her eyes on their hands rather than his face, voice steady but thinner than usual.
“My father was the same. He hit my mother. He hit me. For a long time I thought that was just… normal. Something you were supposed to endure.” A small, quiet breath. “I didn’t have a name for it either until much later. By then it was already over.”
She didn’t go into the night her mother had used Saika. She didn’t mention the blood or the blade or the way everything in her had locked shut afterward. She simply let the shared recognition sit between them—two people who had both learned too young that home could be the most dangerous place.
Anri’s fingers stayed gently over his.
“I’m sorry that happened to you,” she said. “And I’m glad… that you say life got better after. That matters.”
She finally looked up at him, glasses catching the café light, expression open in a way she rarely allowed.
“Thank you for telling me. You didn’t have to.”
Curtis was still for a moment under her hand. When he spoke again, his voice stayed low and even, the same flat practicality he used for most painful things.
“…I see. In a way that’s much worse.” He met her eyes briefly. “I’m so sorry.”
A short pause. He didn’t pull his hand away.
“Both my parents abused me. I have Rh-null blood—I guess it’s rare. When I was four I got hurt real bad. They refused to pay for treatment. Blamed me for it. Made me apologize for getting hurt.”
His gaze drifted toward the window, long black braids shifting slightly against his shoulder.
“They used to whip me with a bullwhip. I still got the scars on my back.”
He said it without drama or self-pity, the same way he had spoken about being abandoned in the forest—like he was simply listing facts that had shaped him rather than asking to be comforted. The café noise continued around them, soft and distant, while the weight of what he had just given her settled across the small table.
Curtis finally glanced back at Anri, mismatched eyes calm but open enough to show he knew exactly how heavy the words were.
Anri’s hand stayed lightly over his.
For a long moment she didn’t speak. The usual quiet distance she kept between herself and other people had thinned, but she still moved carefully around heavy things, the same way she always had.
“…That’s cruel,” she said at last, voice soft and even. “Making a child apologize for being hurt. And a bullwhip…”
She looked down at their hands rather than his face, black bob falling slightly forward.
“I don’t know what to say that wouldn’t sound small.” A quiet breath. “But I’m sorry you went through that. And I’m sorry no one stopped it when you were too young to understand what it was.”
She finally glanced up at him, glasses catching the café light. There was no pity in her expression—only the same careful, steady attention she had given him since the rooftop night, the kind that didn’t demand anything back.
“…Thank you for trusting me with it,” she added, almost too quietly. “You didn’t have to tell me any of that.”
Her fingers remained gently over his, a small, unspoken point of contact she didn’t rush to take away.
Curtis gave a small, almost self-aware breath and glanced down at their hands before looking back at her.
“It’s crazy. I always get looks when I mention it. But it doesn’t bother me. I’ve long since moved on.” A short pause, the corner of his mouth lifting just slightly. “And hey—I got through that, and right now I’m having milk tea with someone who doesn’t make me feel like running away.”
Anri’s fingers tightened almost imperceptibly over his.
For a moment she didn’t know what to do with the quiet honesty of it. Most people either pulled away from her or tried too hard to fill the silence. He did neither. He simply stayed, and said she made the staying feel possible.
“…I’m glad,” she said softly.
She kept her eyes on the table for a second longer, then looked up at him again, the usual careful distance in her expression thinned by something warmer and more uncertain.
“I don’t… usually know what to say when people share things like that. Most of the time I just listen. But I’m glad I don’t make you want to leave.” A small, quiet breath. “I don’t want you to feel like you have to run. Not from this.”
She didn’t pull her hand away. She simply let the contact remain, steady and unassuming, while the milk tea between them slowly cooled.
Curtis stretched in his seat, long black braids shifting across his shoulders—
—and a voice slid into his mind, sweet and wrong.
Cut him.
Make him love you.
Anri’s expression tightened almost immediately. Her fingers withdrew from his hand by a fraction, and a faint, uncomfortable tension settled across her shoulders. She had heard it too.
Curtis’s mismatched eyes narrowed slightly. He studied her for a second, then spoke in a low, even voice.
“I see. That must be troublesome.”
Anri’s gaze flicked up to him, startled.
“Umm—what does—”
Instead of answering out loud, Curtis pulled out his phone, typed quickly, and turned the screen toward her.
I don’t know what I just heard.
I’m not going to question you or invade your secrets.
But if something is bothering you in your mind, it’s fine if you need to leave.
Just promise me you’ll be safe going home.
He held the phone steady so she could read it clearly, expression calm, offering her a clean exit without demand or curiosity he wasn’t entitled to.
Anri stared at the message.
For a moment the usual soft composure she wore like armor wavered. Her eyes moved from the screen to his face, then back again, as if trying to measure how much he had actually perceived and how gently he was choosing not to push.
“…You heard it,” she whispered, almost too quietly to catch.
She didn’t deny the voice. She didn’t explain it. She simply sat there with the weight of Saika’s words still lingering between them, and the rare, careful kindness of someone who had noticed and still given her the choice to walk away.
Curtis locked his phone and slipped it back into his pocket. The weight of what he had overheard still sat between them, but he didn’t press it.
“Let’s go out for a walk, Anri-chan.” His voice stayed low and even. “Let’s go do something. Because I honestly didn’t mean to hear that, and I hope I didn’t make you feel uncomfortable.”
Anri looked at him for a moment longer, the discomfort from Saika’s voice still faintly visible behind her glasses. Then, quietly, she reached out and took his hand.
Her fingers closed around his with careful certainty.
“…Okay,” she said softly. “I’d like that.”
A small pause. She didn’t let go.
“You didn’t make me uncomfortable. Not in the way you think. I just… wasn’t expecting anyone else to hear it.” She glanced up at him, then away again. “Thank you for not asking.”
Curtis gave a short nod and stood, keeping her hand in his as they left Chatime. The evening air had cooled. They walked without a fixed destination at first, the city’s noise settling around them in a more distant way than before. Anri stayed close, her grip light but steady, as if the contact itself was helping hold the earlier tension at bay.
Eventually the streets narrowed and the light dimmed. They passed under a low concrete underpass, the sudden quiet of the space cutting off the broader noise of Ikebukuro. A few distant footsteps echoed, then faded. For the moment, it was just the two of them beneath the overpass, the city’s glow reduced to thin strips of light at either end.
Anri slowed, still holding his hand, and looked around the secluded stretch of concrete and shadow.
Under the dim concrete of the underpass, Curtis turned to face her fully. His voice stayed low and even.
“Something wants you to cut me. I heard it.” A short pause. “Why not? Just do it.”
Anri’s eyes widened behind her glasses. Her hand tightened in his before she could stop it.
“…What?” The word came out thin. “Curtis-kun, I—I can’t. That’s not— you don’t understand what you’re asking.”
Curtis held her gaze without flinching.
“Just trust me on this, Anri. Everything will be okay.”
Before she could argue further, he released her hand and reached up with his other, pushing the sleeve of his white school shirt up past the elbow.
The skin underneath was a map of old violence.
Pale, raised scars crossed his forearm in overlapping lines—some thin and precise, others wider and more brutal. A few looked like they had been made by blades. Others suggested something harsher, repeated over time. The muscle beneath remained solid, but the surface told a clear story of years that had not been kind.
Anri’s breath caught.
“Curtis-kun… your arm—”
She stared, the soft composure she usually wore fracturing into something closer to quiet shock. Her fingers hovered in the air for a second as if she wanted to reach out and didn’t know whether she was allowed.
“…There’s so many,” she whispered. “These aren’t from the other day. These are… old.”
She finally looked up at his face, eyes searching his, the weight of Saika’s earlier whisper suddenly feeling even heavier against the evidence written across his skin.
“Why would you show me this? Why would you ask me to cut you when you’ve already been hurt like this?”
Curtis kept his sleeve rolled up, the lattice of old scars pale under the underpass light.
“These scars aren’t from intentional self-infliction. Each one has a story. I’ll tell you about them later.” His mismatched eyes stayed steady on hers. “But trust me… and cut me.”
Anri’s breathing had gone shallow. Saika was already pressing against the inside of her awareness, eager, hungry, whispering the same command as before. For a long second she stood frozen between the blade’s demand and the calm way Curtis was offering his arm.
Then, with a trembling inhale, she drew Saika.
The original blade slid free with a cold, familiar weight. Anri’s eyes flickered with a faint red sheen as she brought it across his forearm in one clean, shallow slash.
Blood welled immediately.
The moment the edge kissed his skin, Saika saw.
A surge of something vast and furious slammed into the blade’s awareness—an impression of black wings, of battlefield sovereignty, of a presence that did not belong to any ordinary human. There was no name attached to it that Saika recognized, only the absolute certainty that this one could not be claimed. The mark on him rejected possession the way stone rejected water.
Saika’s voice tore through Anri’s mind, no longer sweet.
Don’t cut him again!!!
Anri flinched hard, the blade nearly slipping in her grip. Her eyes widened behind her glasses as the usual endless chorus of love and cut collapsed into something closer to alarm.
She stared at Curtis’s arm—at the fresh line of blood against the older scars—then up at his face, breathing unevenly.
“…It told me to stop,” she whispered, stunned. “Saika never tells me to stop. It always wants more.”
Her hands were shaking around the hilt.
“What are you?”
Curtis looked at the fresh cut on his arm, then at Anri, and gave a small, calm smile.
“I’m human, if that’s what you’re asking.”
Before she could process the answer, he reached out and took Saika from her hands.
The moment his fingers closed around the hilt, the temperature in the underpass seemed to drop. Anri’s breath caught. She knew better than almost anyone what the blade was capable of—possession, infection, the way it could slip into a person’s will through the smallest opening. Watching someone else hold it never felt safe.
Curtis lowered his gaze to the sword.
In the quiet of his own mind, his voice was cold and absolute.
You won’t speak while I am around.
You will let her be herself.
None of this cutting nonsense.
And if you don’t listen, I’ll stab myself with you—and she will erase you.
You also won’t tell her what you saw.
Understand?
For a heartbeat the blade resisted, ancient hunger and fury coiling against the command. Then the presence of the mark on him—the thing Saika had glimpsed and could not name—pressed down like a verdict. The sword went still. The endless whispers of love and cut fell silent in a way Anri had never felt before.
Saika obeyed.
Anri stared at him, face pale, hands still half-raised as if she might need to snatch the blade back or run.
“…Curtis-kun.” Her voice was thin, tight with real fear. “What are you doing? You shouldn’t—Saika doesn’t just cut. It can take people. It can change them. If it decides it wants you—”
She swallowed, eyes fixed on the way he held the original blade so casually.
“Why did it go quiet? It never goes quiet like that. What did you do?”
Curtis turned the hilt of Saika back toward her without ceremony.
Anri took the blade carefully, as if it might still lash out, and slid it away into the place only she could reach. The moment it left his hand, the oppressive stillness in the underpass eased, though the memory of how completely the sword had gone silent remained sharp between them.
Curtis rolled his sleeve back down over the fresh cut and the older scars.
“Can I walk you home?” he asked, voice low. “Or at least part of the way. We don’t have to talk.”
Anri looked at him for a long second, still pale, still unsettled by what she had just witnessed. Then she gave a small nod.
“…Okay. I’d like that.”
They left the underpass together. The city’s noise returned in layers—distant traffic, neon, the low murmur of evening crowds. For a while they walked in silence, the space between them quieter than before but no longer sharp with immediate fear.
It was Anri who eventually broke it.
Her voice was soft, careful, the same tone she used when approaching something fragile.
“…How did you hear it?” she asked. “Saika. No one else has ever… heard it like that. Not without being cut first.”
A few steps later, she added another question, quieter still.
“And when you held it… why did it stop? It’s never done that for anyone. Not even for me.”
She didn’t look at him directly as she spoke, black bob framing her face as she watched the sidewalk ahead. The questions hung in the evening air—curious, cautious, and still missing the one piece she had no way of knowing.
Curtis walked a few more steps in silence before answering, long black braids shifting against his shoulders as he chose his words with care.
“…Well. I told you my parents abused me and abandoned me. But they abandoned me in the Appalachian forest.” His voice stayed even. “There are things in there that I can’t explain. Or I can—but you might think I’m lying.”
A short pause.
“Either way. My brother Justice and I came across this woman out there, and she branded us. Ever since then I’ve been able to hear and interact with spirits, cryptids… cursed objects. More easily than most people.”
He left it there. No mention of the white smoke that answered him like a limb. No name for the mark. No description of the presence Saika had flinched from. Only the limited version that explained enough of what she had witnessed without opening the rest.
Anri’s steps slowed slightly. She looked at him, the evening light catching on her glasses.
“…I didn’t know you had a brother,” she said softly.
Curtis gave a small, almost distant nod.
“Yeah. Justice. I met him in the forest not long after I was left there. He was abandoned too—different reasons, same result. Two kids trying not to die in a place that doesn’t care whether you make it out.” A quiet breath. “We kept each other alive. Taught each other what we could. For a long time it was just the two of us and whatever was watching from the trees.”
He didn’t say what had eventually happened to Justice. He simply let the name and the shared survival sit in the air between them, another carefully measured piece of the truth.
Anri watched him for a moment longer, the usual soft reserve in her expression thinned by something more complicated—recognition, caution, and the quiet weight of realizing how much of his life had been shaped by things most people would never believe.
Anri was quiet for several steps after he finished.
The evening noise of Ikebukuro felt farther away than it should have. She kept her eyes on the sidewalk, processing the shape of what he had given her—an abandoned child, a forest that wasn’t empty, a brother who had survived it with him, and a branding that had left him able to hear things like Saika.
“…That’s a lot,” she said at last, voice soft. “To carry by yourself.”
She glanced up at him, then away again.
“I’m glad you weren’t completely alone out there. That you had Justice.” A small pause. “You talk about him the same way you talked about the scars. Like he’s part of the reason you can stand here and say it without falling apart.”
Her fingers tightened lightly around the strap of her bag.
“I won’t ask you to explain the parts you’re not ready to. But… thank you for telling me this much. Most people would have stopped at ‘I don’t want to talk about it.’”
She walked a little closer to him as they continued, the space between them quieter and less guarded than before.
“I’m still here,” she added, almost too quietly. “Even after hearing all of that.”
Curtis looked at her for a long moment, mismatched eyes steady in the low light of the underpass.
“You’re easy to talk to, Anri-chan.” His voice was quieter than before. “And I have a good tell of character. I had a feeling that if I revealed a piece of my past to you, you wouldn’t use it as leverage against me. Of any kind.”
A short pause.
“No. Instead you stood there and listened. With no ulterior motives.”
Anri’s expression softened in a way she rarely allowed anyone to see. She didn’t answer right away. She simply reached out, took his hand again—this time with more certainty—and turned toward the mouth of the underpass.
Without a word, she led him forward.
Their footsteps echoed once against the concrete, then softened as they stepped out of the shadowed enclosure and back into the open evening. The city’s light returned in gradual layers: neon, streetlamps, the distant movement of people who knew nothing of brands or blades or abandoned children in deep forests.
Anri kept hold of his hand as they emerged, walking a half-step ahead, as if guiding him out of more than just the underpass. Out of the sealed, careful space he so often trapped himself inside. Out of the habit of offering only fragments and then waiting for the world to prove it would use them against him.
She didn’t look back to check if he was following.
She simply held on, and led him into the light.
They stood together in the cooling evening for a quiet moment longer, the weight of everything said under the underpass settling into something softer between them. The hour had grown late.
Curtis pulled out his phone, tapped through a few screens, and confirmed a ride. Within a couple of minutes a clean, unmarked car pulled up to the curb. He checked the plate, then opened the rear door for her.
“Just tell her where home is. She’ll drop you off.”
Anri hesitated, glancing at the car.
“…Isn’t this pricey?”
Curtis gave a small shrug, long black braids shifting against his shoulders.
“While my money isn’t unlimited, I still have quite a bit to sit on. I worked a lot in the States before coming here. But no—this isn’t expensive. Regular price if you do the conversion from yen to USD.”
Anri looked at him for a second longer, then stepped forward and hugged him. It was brief, careful, but genuine.
“…Thank you,” she said against his shoulder. “For the tea. For listening. For… all of it. And for the safe ride.”
She let go, slid into the back seat, and the door closed with a soft click. The car pulled away from the curb.
As the city lights began to slide past the window, something tugged at the edge of her thoughts.
The way he spoke.
The calm way he had offered safety without demanding anything back.
The exact phrasing—
Just promise me you’ll be safe going home.
Anri’s fingers tightened slightly in her lap.
That was what the Tech Rider had said to her in the alley. Almost word for word. The sealed helmet, the filtered voice, the white smoke, the man who had pulled her out of the fight with the Black Rider and asked for nothing…
Her train of thought was cut off by the female driver’s voice from the front seat, warm and lightly amused.
“You have such a nice boyfriend. He paid extra on the app for a female driver—and I can see why.”
Anri blinked, pulled out of the realization mid-formation. A faint flush rose behind her glasses as she looked toward the front.
“…Ah—he’s not… we’re not…” She trailed off, then settled on something quieter and more honest. “He’s just… someone I trust.”
She turned her face back toward the window, the half-formed connection between Curtis and the Tech Rider still sitting unresolved in her chest as the car carried her home.
